#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=January 12, 2017
|author=Jesse Hassenger
|title=A literal monster truck is far from the stupidest thing about Monster Trucks
|work=The Onion AV Club
|url=http://www.avclub.com/review/literal-monster-truck-far-stupidest-thing-about-mo-248111
|page=
|passage=Mechanically, operating this hybrid vehicle is sort of a cross between driving a car and taming an animal, which means the movie treats the audience to the sight of a man (pretending to be a teenager) driving a '''literal''' monster truck in a field next to a woman (also pretending to be a teenager) riding a horse.}}
# [[follow|Following]] the letter or exact words; not [[free]]; not taking [[liberties]]
#: {{ux|en|A '''literal''' reading of the law would prohibit it, but that is clearly not the intent.}}
# {{lb|en|theology}} {{lb|en|broadly}} That which generally assumes that the [[plainest]] reading of a given [[scripture]] is correct but which allows for metaphor where [[context]] indicates it; {{lb|en|specifically}} following the {{w|historical-grammatical method}} of [[biblical]] interpretation
#* {{quote-book|en|year= 1998|author= w:Kevin Vanhoozer|title= Is There a Meaning in This Text?|chapter= 6. Redeeming the Text: The Rationality of Literary Acts|page= 311|pageurl= https://books.google.com/books?id=2LES8XbNJkUC&pg=PA311#v=onepage&q&f=false|text= It is most important to distinguish literalistic from '''literal''' interpretation. [...] ‘Literalistic’ interpretation is like word-for-word translation that yields the verbally exact or ‘formally equivalent’ versions but also runs the risk of overlooking the main (illocutionary) point. '''Literal''' interpretation, on the other hand, is more like a translation that strives for dynamic equivalence and yields the literary sense.}}
# {{lb|en|uncommon}} Consisting of, or expressed by, [[letter]]s (of an [[alphabet]]); using [[literation]].
#: {{ux|en|a '''literal''' equation}}
# {{lb|en|of a person}} [[unimaginative|Unimaginative]]; [[matter-of-fact]]
# {{lb|en|proscribed}} {{non-gloss|Used non-literally as an intensifier; see [[literally]] for usage notes.}}
#: {{ux|en|Telemarketers are the '''literal''' worst.}}
